<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinand II of Aragon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1452">1452</a>–<a href="/years/1516">1516</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-trastamara">House of Trastámara</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinand II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> was king of Aragon whose marriage to Isabella of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> united the two crowns. Together they completed the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> in 1492, sponsored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s voyage and expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, laying the foundations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon.jpg" alt="Ferdinand II of Aragon" width="313" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Sittow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michel_Sittow_004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferdinand was born on 10 March 1452 in Sos del Rey Católico in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-aragon">John II of Aragon</a>, of a cadet branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-trastamara">House of Trastámara</a>, by his second wife, Juana Enríquez. On 19 October 1469 he married Isabella, the half-sister and heir presumptive of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-castile">Henry IV of Castile</a>, in Valladolid. The two were second cousins, and they married under a clear prenuptial agreement on sharing power and the joint motto tanto monta, monta tanto. When Isabella succeeded her brother he became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> through his wife, and the two had first to fight a civil war against the supporters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joanna-la-beltraneja">Joanna la Beltraneja</a>, which they won swiftly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Ferdinand succeeded his father in 1479, the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and the territories of the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> were united in a personal union, as separate units under the same monarchs. Their early joint rule saw the completion in 1492 of the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. In March of that year they issued the Alhambra Decree, ordering all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to convert or leave, and they commissioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> of 1494 divided the world beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> between Portugal and Castile. In 1502 Ferdinand ended the religious freedom guaranteed to Muslims by the Treaty of Granada of 1491 and forced them to convert or leave.</p><p>The later part of his life was largely taken up with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> against the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-fernandez-de-cordoba">Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</a> won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> from the French by 1504. After Isabella's death on 26 November 1504 Ferdinand became governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> for their daughter Joanna, but the Castilian nobility rebuffed him and in the Treaty of Villafáfila of 1506 he gave way to her husband Philip. In 1506 he married Germaine of Foix. Philip's death in September 1506 returned him to power, and in 1507 he had Joanna confined and ruled Castile in her name. He took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-cambrai">League of Cambrai</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1511">Holy League</a> against Louis XII, and in 1512 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ferdinand died on 23 January 1516 at Madrigalejo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a>, and is entombed at the Capilla Real in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, beside Isabella, Joanna and Philip. Joanna inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> and was nominally his successor, but power soon passed to her son Charles, who inherited the Spanish and Italian lands of his maternal grandparents as well as the Austrian and Burgundian lands of his father's family. The Catholic Monarchs centralised the crown's power, at least in name, and prepared the ground for a strong military. Legally, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Aragon remained two kingdoms until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nueva-planta-decrees">Nueva Planta decrees</a> of 1707 to 1716, though Ferdinand is considered the de facto first king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferdinand II of Aragon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Ferdinand II</b> the Crown joined Castile in the conquest of Granada, completed in 1492, and Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba won Naples back from France at Cerignola and the Garigliano in 1503.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Isabella I and <b>Ferdinand II</b> fought the Granada War of 1482–1492 with large armies, siege artillery and an efficient supply system, experience that Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba carried into the Italian Wars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Isabella I and <b>Ferdinand II</b> exploited a split between Abu al-Hasan, his brother Muhammad al-Zaghal and his son Muhammad XII, known as Boabdil, who rebelled against his father in 1482.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The marriage of Isabella I of Castile and <b>Ferdinand II of Aragon</b> in 1469 had joined their crowns in the person of the monarch, while each kingdom kept its own laws and institutions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The marriage of <b>Ferdinand II of Aragon</b> and Isabella I of Castile in 1469 then joined the Crowns of Aragon and Castile under the Catholic Monarchs, the foundation of the Spanish monarchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles VIII of France entered the city in February 1495 almost unopposed but soon withdrew; Louis XII and <b>Ferdinand II of Aragon</b> agreed in 1500 to partition the kingdom, then fell out, and the Spanish commander Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba, the 'Great Captain', won the Battle of Cerignola,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Palacio de los Sada, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Casa de Santa María. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.5,-1.22&amp;year=1493" aria-label="Palacio de los Sada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4521 653 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ferdinand II of Aragon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4971 923h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4971 923h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.5,-1.22&amp;year=1493">What was here in 1493? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
